## Bootstrap 6

This app runs Bootstrap <%= facts[:bootstrap_version] %>. Don't write Bootstrap 6 markup (`dialog`, `drawer`, `menu`, `md:col-6`, `btn-solid theme-primary`) unless the app is being moved to Bootstrap 6: none of it works on <%= facts[:bootstrap_version] %>. The gem's generators and helpers produce Bootstrap 5 markup.

To move the app to Bootstrap 6:

1. Install the upstream migration skill: `<%= v6::SKILL_COMMAND %>`. Follow it alongside this guide.
2. Run `rails g bootstrap:doctor --v6 --json`. It lists every Bootstrap 5 class, data attribute, Sass import and JavaScript call that changes, with file, line and replacement, under `v6_migration`.
3. Switch the Bootstrap assets before changing markup, because the vendored files and `bootstrap:install` are Bootstrap 5. `rails g bootstrap:cdn --bootstrap=6` prints the prerelease CDN tags. Re-running `bootstrap:layout`, `bootstrap:themed` or a snippet generator writes Bootstrap 5 markup again.
4. The helpers in "View helpers" render Bootstrap 5 markup at runtime, so the scan can't see their output. Replace them with Bootstrap 6 markup in the views that use them.
5. v6 changes the spacing scale (`p-3` is smaller), so check spacing utilities by eye; the scan doesn't flag them.

## Markup to avoid

These come from older Bootstrap versions and do nothing on this one. `bootstrap:doctor` reports them, with file and line, under `legacy_markup`. Plain names like `close` or `well` may be the app's own CSS classes, so check those before changing them.

Data attributes: <%= legacy::DATA_ATTRIBUTES.map { |name| "`data-#{name}`" }.join(", ") %> become `data-bs-*`.

| Old class | Use instead |
|---|---|
